Mechanism (read at source, both cited lines are new/changed by this ticket):
- update_profile_screen.dart:211-213 wraps the screen in `PopScope(canPop:false,
  onPopInvokedWithResult: (didPop, result) async => _handleBack(context))`.
- update_profile_screen.dart:127-159 `_handleBack` guards re-entry with a `_popping` bool, but
  ONLY for calls that go through `_handleBack` itself (AppBar back tap, hardware back). It does
  NOT know about a pop initiated any other way.
- profile_controller.dart:214-217, on a successful non-desktop save, calls `Get.back(); Get.back();`
  directly (bypassing `_handleBack`/`_popping` entirely) to leave Edit Profile AND its own dialog
  parent. The FIRST `Get.back()` -> `NavigatorState.pop` for the Edit Profile route, which (being
  wrapped in PopScope) synchronously re-enters via `onPopInvokedWithResult` -> `_handleBack`, whose
  `_popping` flag is still false (this pop was never routed through `_handleBack`), so `_handleBack`
  evaluates isDirty()==false (fields already match post-getUserInfo()) and calls
  `Navigator.pop(context)` a SECOND time — while the navigator is still mid-pop from the first call
  (`_debugLocked==true`), tripping the framework's own re-entrancy assertion.
- Net effect this run: the app still lands on Home (both pops complete), so no user-visible stuck
  state was observed live — but every real, non-desktop Save from a clean-after-reload state throws
  this uncaught exception, which is a real Crashlytics-worthy assertion failure in production
  (recorded once for a non-debug build), not a display-only crash.

breaks_ac: true — EP2 (Save success -> exit) and the same-ticket PopScope hardware-back gate this
QA was specifically asked to verify are the ones producing this reentrancy; the fix-cycle's own
"_popping" comment explicitly reasoned about ONE re-entry path (repeated _handleBack calls) and did
not cover this second one (a pop initiated by the controller directly).
